<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Which destinations plain `http://` may reach.

  The app already has a transport policy of its own — `permitsCleartext` in
  `data/HubAddress.kt` — which allows plain http to this machine and to the LAN,
  because a hub on a dev machine or a LAN has no certificate. That policy was
  only ever half the answer: an app targeting SDK 28 or later gets a platform
  policy that blocks cleartext by default, and it applies before any of the
  app's code is consulted.

  The emulator settled it. `NetworkSecurityPolicy.isCleartextTrafficPermitted`
  answered **false** for `127.0.0.1`, which means pairing to a hub on the dev
  machine over http — the documented way to set one up, and the one
  `scripts/bootstrap.sh` builds — could not work on Android and never had.
  `CleartextPolicyTest` is that experiment, and it now pins the answer.

  So: loopback, and nothing else.

  `10.0.2.2` is the emulator's alias for the host machine's loopback, which is
  where a hub started by the bootstrap script is listening; without it the
  emulator cannot reach a hub on the machine running it.

  **What this deliberately does NOT do** is open cleartext to the LAN. A
  network-security config can name hosts, not ranges, so the RFC 1918 space
  `permitsCleartext` allows cannot be expressed here. A hub at
  `http://192.168.1.10:8899` is still refused by the platform on Android, and
  the app will report a connection failure rather than a reason — that is a
  known, narrow gap, recorded in README.md, and the fix for an operator who
  needs it is https or an entry below. It is not closed by turning cleartext on
  for everything, which would put the bearer token on the open internet in the
  clear on every other network the phone joins.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false" />

    <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">127.0.0.1</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">localhost</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">10.0.2.2</domain>
    </domain-config>
</network-security-config>
